    "rationale": "Evidence: across iterations 0 to 2 every one of the 180 trials ran off track, and progress stayed at 0.10 to 0.16 whatever the speed. Max_error sat at 0.061 to 0.067 m, just over the 0.06 m limit, so the robot drifts off early in each course rather than failing late. Iteration 1 (kp 4.0, kd 0.3, wide sensors) removed oval line loss but not drift. Iteration 2 lowered kp to 3.5 and base_speed to 1.2 and scored worse (52.03 vs 56.08 and 54.27). The overshoot hypothesis is therefore not supported. The trend points the other way: lower kp has produced worse drift, so the robot needs more correction authority through the bends. Changes: (1) kp 3.5 to 5.0 for stronger correction at the 0.05 m outer sensors. (2) kd 0.3 to 0.4, the lever the feedback named, to damp the higher gain and keep steering_oscillation from rising. (3) base_speed 1.2 to 1.4, a middle value so the higher kp is tested at a speed where progress was reasonable. (4) sensor_size 0.016 back to 0.012. Iteration 1 with 0.012 showed no oval line loss on the wide array, and the larger footprint did not help in iteration 2. Sensor positions (x = -0.05 to 0.05 at y = 0.05) and antisymmetric weights are kept, so the steering sign is unchanged. ki stays at 0.1 to cancel the fixed motor mismatch. Expected result: max_error below 0.06 on the oval and s-bend, progress above 0.3, and line_loss_events near 0. Completion in 3 s is unlikely at 1.4 m/s. Hypotheses to check: (a) kp is too low, not too high, and more correction keeps max_error under 0.06; (b) kd 0.4 keeps steering_oscillation below about 0.14 at the higher gain. Next race: if progress rises but max_error is still above 0.06, try kp 6.0 before changing speed. If oscillation rises above 0.2, lower kp to 4.5 and keep kd 0.4.",
